<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagabhata II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 805–833</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagabhata II was a king of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 9th century. Around 816 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayudha-dynasty">Ayudha dynasty</a>, a client of the Pala Empire, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, which became the Pratihara capital. His reign renewed the long struggle between the Pratiharas, the Palas and the Rashtrakutas for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nagabhata II was the son of the Pratihara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> and Queen Sundari-Devi, and succeeded his father on the throne. Early in his career he faced a large Pala army, said to have had 50,000 elephants, led by King Dharmapala himself at Mungar, and emerged victorious. The Chatsu inscription of 813 CE, set up by his Guhila feudatory Baladitya, recalls how Shankaragana Guhila, fighting for Vatsaraja, had defeated Bhata, the ruler of Gauda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Gwalior inscription credits Nagabhata with victories over the rulers of Sindhu, Andhra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, the Matsyas, Vatsas, Malavas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirata">Kiratas</a> and Anartas, and over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. He defeated the Saindhava ruler Ranaka I and conquered western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>. He was in turn defeated by the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, but he recovered Malwa, took the Indo-Gangetic Plain as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> from the Palas, and again checked the Muslims in the west.</p><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> he took the imperial titles Paramabhattaraka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a> and Paramesvara. Kannauj became the centre of the Gurjara Pratihara state, which at its height between 836 and 910 covered much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>An inscription of his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a> praises him as a king who performed a series of religious ceremonies according to the custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> families, and he is said to have been a devotee of Bhagavati. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramabhadra">Ramabhadra</a>. Some earlier historians identified him with Ama, a king who according to Jain accounts died in 832-833 CE, and the end of his reign around 833 rests on that identification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagabhata II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Nagabhata II</b> took Kannauj from the Ayudha dynasty around 816 and made it the imperial capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, yet managed to place Chakrayudha on the throne of Kannauj after both rivals withdrew; around 800 the Rashtrakuta Govinda III marched north and received the submission of both Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and <b>Nagabhata II</b> defeated Dharmapala near Monghyr and took Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Govinda III repeated the feat c. 800, defeating <b>Nagabhata II</b>, while Dharmapala and his client Chakrayudha of Kannauj submitted; Rashtrakuta inscriptions claim that his horses drank from the Himalayan streams.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 816 <b>Nagabhata II</b> of the Gurjara-Pratihara defeated Chakrayudha and took Kannauj, which became the Pratihara capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayudha-dynasty">Ayudha dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.03,79.92&amp;year=819" aria-label="Kannauj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Nagabhata II"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4236 5220h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4236 5220h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=27.03,79.92&amp;year=819">What was here in 819 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
